Data structures
===============

All of these are file-scoped (my) package variables unless noted. "File" =
the .pm that declares them.

Detector.pm -- the device registry
----------------------------------

- %model_table : label -> model object. Populated by Detector::init,
  which constructs one instance of every model class and calls
  Detector->register($mod) for each (the models do not self-register). label
  is the device-table name (cisco-ios, generic, ...).

- @dev_order : ordered list of dev_ids in device-table order. The
  sequential fetch phase iterates this; the maintenance modes use it to list
  devices deterministically.

- %dev_id_table : dev_id -> source line number. Used to detect and
  report a duplicate dev_id.

- %dev_info_table : dev_id -> a hashref describing the device:

      {
        model       => $mod,          # the model object (from %model_table)
        dev_host    => $dev_host,     # hostname or IP (2nd field of the device line)
        dev_opt_tab => $dev_opt_tab,  # the merged per-device option hash (see 3.2)
        file        => $file,         # source device-table file (for messages)
        num         => $num,          # source line number
        line        => $line,         # the raw source line (for messages, masked)
      }

- The directory-allowlist structures (populated from directory: lines):
  - %dir_allow_tag : $TAG -> { type =>
    repository|template|fetch_run|report|lock, path => <abs path> }. The $TAG
    indirection a device value uses.
  - %dir_allow_path : normalised allowed path -> type, for membership tests.
  - $dir_allow_present : true once any directory: line has been seen (switches
    the allowlist from "off/opt-in" to "enforced").
  - @dir_allow_rows : ordered [type, tag, path] for --list-allowed-dirs.
  - @dir_allow_errors : [reason, ...] for each rejected directory: line; a
    non-empty list aborts the run before any fetch (Security mechanisms) and is
    what --list-allowed-dirs lints.
  - $dir_allow_fetch_run_none / $dir_allow_report_none : set by directory:
    fetch_run none / directory: report none (disable that capability).
  - %warned_unlisted_dir : dedup set for the "no allowlist entry" warning, reset
    per device-table load.

- @SYSTEM_DIRS : the list of directories (/bin, /etc, /usr/lib, ...)
  that are never acceptable as a repository/template/fetch_run directory,
  tested by _system_dir_path().

- $DEV_ID_RE = qr/^[\w.-]+$/ : the allowed dev_id character set.
  A dev_id is also rejected if it is only dots.

- $default_lock_dir : <canonical dir of fetchconfig.pl>/.lock, set by
  Detector::init($log, $lock_dir) from the driver.

- $lock_dir_failed : set if any device's lock dir could not be created
  or used; the driver turns this into exit 10 after the run.

$dev_opt_tab -- the per-device option hash
------------------------------------------

The single most important structure for an extender. It is a flat hashref:
option name -> string value, built in phase 1 by merging the model's
default: options under the device line's own options (device wins). Every
option in The device table format appears here by its bare name (repository,
pass, keep,
debug, lock, to, ...). Models and the pipeline read it only through
the Abstract accessors, never by direct hash access:

- dev_option($tab, $name) -- the effective value, with a $TAG expanded to
  its real path for directory-valued options, and with timeout-family options
  validated (a non-positive-integer timeout/fetch_timeout/banner_timeout/
  prompt_settle_ms is ignored so the documented default applies).
- dev_option_raw($tab, $name) -- the effective value with no $TAG
  expansion (used by the allowlist enforcement, which must see the raw value).
- dev_option_flag($tab, $name, $default) -- a boolean option
  (on/off), e.g. debug, lock, changes_only (where applicable).
- dev_option_posint($tab, $name, $default, $allow_zero) -- a validated
  non-negative/positive integer, e.g. lock_retry (0 allowed),
  lock_retry_time (>=1).

To add an option: decide whether it is directory-valued (add it to
%DIR_VALUED_OPTION in Abstract so $TAG expands), numeric (read with
dev_option_posint), boolean (dev_option_flag), or plain (dev_option).
No parse change is needed -- parse_options accepts any key=value; the
option "exists" as soon as something reads it.

Mailer.pm -- the result record
------------------------------

- @results : one hashref per device outcome, pushed by
  record_result(%args). Under -P every field is serialised through the result
  sink; the .status file gets only a derived subset (backup_start/end_date,
  backup_start/end_time, backup_duration_sec, backup_state,
  backup_changed), not these fields verbatim. The fields:

      {
        dev_id     => ...,   # device id
        dev_host   => ...,   # hostname/IP
        ts         => ...,   # epoch of the fetch start (for Date/Time columns)
        elapsed    => ...,   # seconds the fetch took
        success    => 0|1,   # hard success/failure (undef on a 'skipped' row)
        size       => ...,   # backup size in bytes, or undef
        changed    => 'changed'|'unchanged'|'initial'|'n/a',
        state      => 'skipped' (optional; explicit state wins over success)
        repository => ...,   # so the per-device .status file can be written
        to         => ...,   # device-specific recipient override (the `to=` option)
      }

  The effective State shown in the mail is computed by row_state($row):
  an explicit state (currently only 'skipped') wins; otherwise
  success ? 'success' : 'failed'.

- %email_config : the parsed email: options (from, to, smtp,
  user, password, tls, report_dir, write_report, email_max_diff,
  web_report_url, report_logo, report_days, mask_secrets, footer).

- $rep_url, $rep_email_diffs, $rep_max_diff, $rep_mask : material
  carried from send_summaries into the HTML/text builders (the report link,
  the diffs to embed, the diff cap, the masking flag).

- $MAIL_LOGO_B64 : the inline e-mail header logo (base64 PNG), embedded
  as a multipart/related Content-ID: <fcwlogo> part. Independent of the
  on-disk report's report_logo=.

On-disk structures
------------------

- Backup file :
  <repository>/YYYYMM/YYYYMMDD/<dev_id>/<dev_id>.run.YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS[tz]
  (optionally plus filename_append_suffix). parse_backup_filename() and
  sort_backups_desc() parse and order these by the parsed timestamp
  YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS, never by a plain string sort of the whole name (so the
  timezone token and a suffix cannot disturb the order). Written with
  O_EXCL so two runs completing in the same second cannot silently
  overwrite one another (Security mechanisms).

- .status file : <repository>/<dev_id>.status -- the last-run result
  for that device (written atomically via a dotfile temp + rename -- the temp,
  not the final file, carries the leading dot), consumed by fetchconfig-web and
  the maintenance modes.

- .debug file : <repository>/<dev_id>.debug -- the full session trace
  when debug=on (0600; Debug tracing (debug=on)). Overwritten each run.

- Lock file : <lock_dir>/<sha256(dev_id)>.lock -- a flock holder, never
  unlinked on release (Locking).

------------------------------------------------------------

The device table format
=======================

The device table is a line-oriented text file (one or more, via -devices=;
individual lines can also be injected with -line=). Blank lines and #
comment lines are ignored. There are four line types, recognised by prefix.

Device line
-----------

    <model>  <dev_id>  <host>  <opt1=val1,opt2=val2,...>

- <model> -- a registered model label (e.g. cisco-ios, generic).
- <dev_id> -- the unique device id. Must match ^[\w.-]+$ and must not be
  only dots. It is the directory name under the repository and the key in
  every status/lock/result structure. **A device id is never user-free-form:
  it is validated at load time.**
- <host> -- hostname or IP used to connect.
- the option list -- comma-separated key=value pairs. A value may be
  double-quoted to contain a comma or spaces (e.g. pass="a,b c"). A value
  of the form $TAG is a directory-allowlist tag, expanded for
  directory-valued options.

Example:

    cisco-ios  rtr01  10.0.0.1  user=backup,pass="s3cr3t",enable=en,repository=$REPO1,keep=30

default: line
-------------

    default:  <model>  <opt1=val1,...>

Sets per-model default options. A device line of that model inherits these
unless it overrides them. Merging is per-option (device value wins). A bad
value inherited from a default: line (e.g. a non-numeric keep) rejects
every device that inherits it.

email: line
-----------

    email:  from=...,to=...,smtp=...,...

Configures the summary e-mail. If no email: line is present, no SMTP
connection is ever attempted. Options: from, to, smtp (host or
host:port), user, password, tls (off|starttls|ssl), report_dir,
write_report (on), email_max_diff, web_report_url, report_logo
(FILE|none), report_days, mask_secrets (off to disable), footer.
See README -> EMAIL NOTIFICATIONS for each.

directory: line (the allowlist)
-------------------------------

    directory:  <type>  <$TAG|none>  [<abs-path>]

<type> is repository, template, fetch_run, report, or lock.
Presence of any directory: line turns the allowlist on; thereafter a
device's repository=, template_dir=, and the on_fetch_run program directory
must resolve to an allowed path (directly or via a $TAG). (lock is an accepted
directory: type that lock_dir= can resolve a $TAG through, but lock_dir=
itself is not allowlist-enforced -- it is only $TAG-expanded and
system-directory-checked.) directory: fetch_run none / directory: report none
disable that capability. A directory: line that names a system directory, or is
otherwise malformed, is recorded in @dir_allow_errors and aborts the run
before any fetch. See README -> DIRECTORY ALLOWLIST for the full semantics
and --list-allowed-dirs for the linter.

pw: line (credential aliases)
-----------------------------

    pw:  user  $Alias  <username>
    pw:  pass  $Alias  <secret>

A pw: line registers a named credential alias, the same way a directory: line
registers a $TAG. parse_pw_line validates the kind (user or pass), the
alias name (^\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$  -  $ then a letter or _, never a
leading digit), rejects duplicates and any clash with a directory: $TAG, and
dequotes the value through Abstract::dequote_value; pass values are masked in
logs.

A credential-slot value is interpreted by the first character of its raw token
(Abstract::_expand_pw_alias, reached via _resolve_alias): a leading " is a
quoted, fully-verbatim literal (dequoted, never an alias); a leading $
matching the name grammar is an alias reference (resolved, kind-checked  -
user/snmp_user take user aliases; pass/enable/snmp_community/
snmp_write_community/snmp_authpass/snmp_privpass take pass aliases; an
undefined or wrong-kind alias logs an error and yields undef); anything else is
a
verbatim literal with a first-character-only de-escape (_deescape_first:
leading \$->$, leading \\->\, interior untouched). So the two ways to write a
$-leading literal are \$value or "$value". To keep the quoted-ness available
to the resolver, parse_options stores credential slots' raw token (quotes
intact)  -  it skips dequote_value for the %PW_SLOT_KIND options and the
resolver
does the dequote.

Malformed pw: lines accumulate in @pw_errors (accessors pw_errors /
pw_present / pw_alias / pw_rows); like directory: errors they abort the run
(exit 2) before any fetch. --list-credentials lists the defined aliases (values
masked) and lints them  -  malformed lines plus a cross-table check that every
credential reference resolves to a defined alias of the right kind  -  exiting 2
on
any problem, so fetchconfig-web's validate_table_with_fetchconfig can gate saves
on it.

Options available for all models
--------------------------------

debug, to, on_fetch_run, on_fetch_cat, timezone,
filename_append_suffix, comment, site, and the locking options (lock,
lock_dir, lock_retry, lock_retry_time) are available for every model.
The SNMP inventory options (snmp_community/snmp_write_community/snmp_user,
snmp_version, snmp_port, snmp_timeout, snmp_retries, snmp_max_repetitions,
the v3 snmp_level/snmp_authproto/snmp_authpass/snmp_privproto/snmp_privpass,
and
snmp_inventory/snmp_inventory_level/snmp_inventory_history/snmp_fatal/snmp_inventory_interval)
are
also model-independent -- the collector reads them through dev_option on any
model
(see SNMPInventory.pm and the per-device pipeline). Exception:
snmp_profile_file is read through default_option, so it is default:-only --
the only device-table option restricted to default: lines (a device-line value
is
ignored, with a warning), because the parsed vendor/model file is cached
process-wide. procurve-snmp additionally consumes
snmp_community/snmp_write_community/snmp_timeout/snmp_version as its own
transport credentials via the shared open_snmp_session.
Model-specific options (user, pass, enable, timeout, repository,
keep, fetch_timeout, changes_only, ...) vary by model -- see the per-model
options tables and the model sections in README (the DEVICE OPTIONS section).
Note procurve-snmp no longer reads the shared timeout (9.80): its timeout is
snmp_timeout.

------------------------------------------------------------

Models and the model API
========================

Lifecycle
---------

Each model is a subclass of fetchconfig::model::Abstract. Detector::init
constructs one instance of every model class and calls Detector->register($mod)
for each, which puts it in %model_table under $mod->label. (Models do not
self-register; adding a model means adding its register(...->new($log)) line to
Detector::init.)

Per device, Detector::fetch_device calls $mod->fetch(...), whose default
implementation in Abstract delegates to the model's do_fetch(...). A model
therefore implements do_fetch (and label), and overrides prompt/login
hooks as needed; everything else is inherited.

The shared API (Abstract.pm)
----------------------------

The methods a model (or an extender) relies on. Signatures are indicative;
read Abstract.pm for exact argument lists.

Session / transport
- ssh_open($dev_host, $dev_user, $dev_pass, $dev_timeout, $debug_fh) -- open a
  Net::OpenSSH session (SSH models). Host-key policy (accept any key, keep none)
  is set here.
- open_ssh_debug_file(...) / open_telnet_debug_file($t, $dev_opt_tab, $repo,
  $dev_id, $dev_host) -- when debug=on, open <dev_id>.debug (0600) and attach it
  as the SSH/telnet session log. The telnet helper (9.71) is what gives every
  telnet model debug=; call it right after the connection opens and close() the
  returned handle at session end.
- wait_for_command_prompt(...), expect_enable_prompt(...),
  expect_enable_prompt_paging_auto(...), drain_until_idle(...),
  chat_banner(...), chat_show_conf(...) -- prompt and login helpers. Models
  override prompt_head() / prompt_tail() / interrupt_screens() /
  ssh_extra_opts() to tune behaviour.

Backup write / compare / expire
- dump_config($dev_id, $dev_opt_tab, \@lines) -- write the backup
  (O_EXCL, umask-driven perms, repository dir auto-created), returns
  ($dir, $file).
- find_latest($dev_id, $dev_opt_tab) -- locate the most recent prior backup.
- config_equal(...) / config_equal_ignoring_lines(...) -- compare two
  configs, ignoring volatile lines.
- config_discard($dir, $file) -- remove the just-written backup when it
  equals the previous one, and prune the dir if emptied.
- purge_ancient($dev_id, $dev_opt_tab) -- delete backups beyond keep=N
  (validates keep; Security mechanisms).

Options / secrets / text
- dev_option* accessors ($dev_opt_tab -- the per-device option hash).
- print_secret($t, $secret) -- send a password/secret to the device **without
  logging it** (suspends the debug log around the send, writes a masked
  marker). Every model sends credentials through this; a new model MUST too.
- mask_secrets($line) -- redact pass=/enable=/community=/password=/
  secret=/key=/token=-style values, plus the SNMP secrets
  snmp_community=/snmp_write_community=/snmp_authpass=/snmp_privpass=
  (9.80), before echoing a device-table line to the log.
- stripansi($s), regexp_quote_keep_bytes($s), escape_brackets($s),
  dequote_value($s) -- text helpers (ANSI stripping, byte-preserving regex
  quoting for learned prompts, etc.).

Locking
- lock_dir_resolve, device_lock_path, acquire_device_lock (Locking).

Call graph -- core <-> model <-> Abstract
-----------------------------------------

How control flows into a model and what the model calls back. ( ) = a hook
the model overrides; [ ] = a shared helper the model calls. Arrows point
from caller to callee.

     Detector::fetch_device
       -> Abstract::fetch -> <Model>::do_fetch   (model implements do_fetch)
    
     <Model>::do_fetch calls shared helpers [ ] and model hooks ( ):
       [ssh_open] / Net::Telnet->new         open transport
       [open_ssh_debug_file]/[open_telnet_debug_file]   attach .debug (debug=on)
       (prompt_head)(prompt_tail)(interrupt_screens)    model-tuned prompts/pager
       [wait_for_command_prompt][expect_enable_prompt]
       [chat_banner][chat_show_conf]                     login/show (chat_login is per-model)
       [print_secret]                        send EVERY credential here
       [stripansi][regexp_quote_keep_bytes][escape_brackets]
       [dev_option][dev_option_flag][dev_option_raw][dev_option_posint]
       [dump_config]                         write the backup (O_EXCL)
       => returns (config_dir, config_file) or undef
    
     Core calls Abstract directly (find_latest runs before the fetch):
       [find_latest] [config_equal]/[config_equal_ignoring_lines]
       [config_discard] [purge_ancient]
       [lock_dir_resolve][device_lock_path][acquire_device_lock]
    
     Model overrides (defaults): label() do_fetch() prompt_head('')
       prompt_tail('#$') interrupt_screens(()) ssh_extra_opts(())

Rule of thumb: a model implements label + do_fetch and overrides
only the prompt/transport hooks it needs; it calls the [ ] helpers for
everything else and never reimplements them. These core-driven helpers --
config_equal, config_discard, purge_ancient, locking, and find_latest
(which core calls before the fetch) -- run in core, not the model; a model
just returns the written path.

Adding a model
--------------

Follow How-To-add-a-model (the authoritative recipe) together with the
data-flow context in The per-device pipeline (fetch_device)/The shared API
(Abstract.pm). In short: create fetchconfig/model/Yours.pm
as an Abstract subclass, implement label and do_fetch, send credentials
with print_secret, call open_telnet_debug_file/open_ssh_debug_file so
debug= works, write the config with dump_config, and register the model.
Add it to t/00-compile.t's expectations and the per-model matrix in the
HTML docs. Capture a real session first with tools/record-session.pl
(see README.generic-model).

For a device that speaks a config-template rather than a bespoke dialect,
prefer the generic model and a template -- see README.generic-model; no
Perl is needed.

------------------------------------------------------------

Debug tracing (debug=on)
========================

debug=on writes a 0600 <repository>/<dev_id>.debug session trace, available
for every model (since 9.71). Three mechanisms, all producing the same
file contract:

- SSH models -> open_ssh_debug_file (records the ssh -v trace +
  session).
- Telnet models -> open_telnet_debug_file (attaches the Net::Telnet
  dump_log).
- HTTP / SNMP models -> their own implementation (HTTP request/response;
  SNMP operations + TFTP transfer).

Credentials never reach the log because models send them via print_secret,
which suspends the log around the send. The file still contains configuration
secrets (hashes, community strings), so it is 0600 and should be deleted after
troubleshooting.

