Maintenance / lookup modes (Tools.pm)
=====================================

These are standalone modes: they use phase-1 registration, operate on the
stored backups (never fetch), and exit. Summary (see README command-line
reference for exact behaviour):

Flag                    Purpose
----------------------  --------------------------------------------------------
-g dev                  print the latest backup of dev to stdout (-f file to
                        write it).
-l dev                  list the backups of dev.
-z dev / -Z             find zero-byte backups for one device / all devices; -D
                        also deletes the files, -T previews.
-o / -e                 orphaned-device / empty-directory report; -D deletes, -T
                        previews.
-s dev / -S             suffix check for one / all devices.
--check-template PATH   structural check of a generic-model template; exits 0 ok
                        / 1 errors / 2 file problem. No device table needed; no
                        allowlist applied.
--template-syntax PATH  print a template's # syntax_style: value for a config
                        viewer.
--list-allowed-dirs     print and lint the directory: allowlist.
--clean-lock-dir [N]    remove lock files older than N days (default 30, min 1);
                        -T previews; flock-first so a held lock is never
                        removed.

Exit codes of note: 0 success; 2 allowlist-abort (and a couple of operational
aborts in Tools.pm: -m on Windows, a missing template dir); 10 lock
directory unusable; 255 usage / bad-argument / fatal die (CLI option errors go
through $opt_fail -> die, which exits 255); lookup modes also use 1
(e.g. zero-byte/orphan found).

------------------------------------------------------------

Locking
=======

lock=on serialises concurrent backups of the same device (overlapping cron,
or a web "Backup Now" over a cron run). Mechanism:

- Key: sha256_hex(dev_id); path: <lock_dir>/<hash>.lock.
- Lock dir: lock_dir= (a directory: lock $TAG or a path), else the
  default <dir of fetchconfig.pl>/.lock, created 0700. If it cannot be
  created/used, the run exits 10 at the end.
- Acquire: sysopen(..., O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_NOFOLLOW, 0600) then
  flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB). On contention, retry lock_retry times (default
  5; 0 = skip immediately) at lock_retry_time-second intervals (default 10),
  then record the device as skipped and continue. The process exit code
  is not changed by a lock-skip.
- Release: automatic when the holding process exits (even on crash). The
  lock file is never unlinked on release (unlinking would open a
  two-holders race). --clean-lock-dir removes stale files flock-first.

Works across the forked -P workers and across separate invocations. Requires
a local filesystem (flock over NFS on AIX is unreliable) -- a documented
operator responsibility.

Device-table file lock (<devices>.lock)
---------------------------------------

Separate from the per-device lock above, and about the table file rather than
a device. --rename-device-id rewrites a device-table line in place; a
concurrent fetch run must not parse a table mid-rename. The lock object is a
sidecar <devices>.lock next to each -devices= file (not the file itself:
the rename swaps the table's inode via temp+rename, so a lock on the file's own
descriptor would not cover the swap), handled by fetchconfig::Lock:

- A normal run takes a shared lock (LOCK_SH) while load_device_list
  parses each -devices= file, with a 2 s bounded, non-blocking acquire,
  released before fetching. On timeout it logs and exits 2 rather than parse
  a table a rename is changing.
- --rename-device-id / --replay-db-change take an exclusive lock
  (LOCK_EX), the rename holding it across the re-check, the on-disk sweep and
  the atomic table rewrite.
- If the sidecar cannot be created (e.g. a read-only directory), a run proceeds
  without the lock (fail-open, so locking never breaks an existing
  deployment); a rename refuses (fail-closed, for safety).

Like the per-device lock this is flock-based, so the same local-filesystem
requirement applies. NB: on AIX flock is emulated via per-process fcntl
locks, so contention is only meaningful between processes (a run vs a
rename), which is the only case that matters here.

------------------------------------------------------------

Security mechanisms
===================

Trust model (read this first)
-----------------------------

- The device table is the credential store and a code-execution surface.
  Whoever can write it already holds every device password and, via
  on_fetch_run, can run a program as the fetchconfig user. The security
  mechanisms below defend against content mistakes and external attackers,
  not against a hostile table author -- that is out of scope by construction.
- fetchconfig-web split. In the fetchconfig-web deployment, content is
  read as the unprivileged nobody user (-l/-g) and backups are written
  as root via sudo (-g/-o). This is why repository files/directories are
  deliberately umask-readable, not owner-only (see 9.6) -- an unprivileged
  reader must be able to read them.

Credential handling
-------------------

- print_secret sends passwords to the device without logging them
  (suspends the session log around the send, leaves a masked marker). Every
  model uses it.
- mask_secrets (Abstract, for device-table lines echoed to the log) redacts
  pass=/enable=/community=/password=/passphrase=/secret=/key=/token=-prefixed
  values and the SNMP secrets
snmp_community=/snmp_write_community=/snmp_authpass=/snmp_privpass=
  (9.80), quoted values included, so a malformed table line in an error message
  does not leak a credential. (Detector has its own, narrower masker for the
lines
  it logs: it covers pass=/enable=/community= and the four snmp_* secrets,
  but not quoted values -- not a copy of Abstract's.)
- mask_line (Report, for config content in the HTML report and the
  mailed diffs) redacts secret N <hash> / password N <hash> forms. It is
  best-effort and biases toward masking (a false positive hides a non-secret
  in the diff view; never the reverse).

Command execution -- on_fetch_run
---------------------------------

on_fetch_run is executed without a shell: the configured value is split
into argv with Text::ParseWords::shellwords and run with the list form of
system (system { $argv[0] } @argv), which goes to execvp() directly.
Shell metacharacters (;, |, $(...), backticks, >) are passed to the
program as literal text, not interpreted. The program must not live in a
system directory. A start failure, signal, or non-zero exit is logged.

Path safety
-----------

- dev_id whitelist ^[\w.-]+$ (dots-only rejected) -- prevents a device
  id from escaping the repository via ../.
- Directory allowlist -- when a directory: section exists, repository=,
  template_dir=, and the on_fetch_run program directory must resolve under an
  allowed path; paths are checked for /../, /./, absolute form, and legal
  characters; a $TAG is expanded. Off-list -> device skipped. (lock_dir= is
  not allowlist-enforced -- it is only $TAG-expanded and
system-directory-checked.)
- System-directory rejection (_system_dir_path) -- repository=,
  template_dir=, the on_fetch_run program directory and lock_dir= may never
  be /bin, /etc, /usr/lib, ... or the root, with or without an allowlist. A
  directory: line that declares one is rejected at parse time.
- Abort on a broken allowlist -- if any directory: line failed to parse,
  the run aborts (exit 2) before any fetch, rather than continuing with a
  silently reduced allowlist (fail-closed).
- O_EXCL backup write -- dump_config creates the backup exclusively, so
  two runs finishing in the same one-second filename window cannot silently
  overwrite each other (the second is refused and reported).

Locking
-------

See Locking -- O_NOFOLLOW on the lock file, 0600 lock files, 0700 lock dir,
flock-based, never-unlink-on-release, flock-first cleanup.

File permissions
----------------

Backup files and their directories, and the .status file, are created with
the process umask -- deliberately not forced owner-only, because the
fetchconfig-web nobody reader must read them (9.1). Restrict the repository
directory yourself if the host is shared. The .debug file is 0600
(it can contain secrets even though passwords are masked). Parallel-fetch
worker scratch files are owner-only (they live in a private temp dir).

Mail
----

- SMTP header-injection -- from, subject, and every recipient have
  CR/LF stripped before the headers are written.
- No plaintext fallback -- tls=starttls|ssl never silently downgrades to
  an unencrypted send; if TLS fails the mail is skipped and logged.

Deliberate NON-mitigations (do not "fix" these blindly)
-------------------------------------------------------

- Repository is umask-readable by design (9.6) -- not a permissions bug.

------------------------------------------------------------

Notification subsystem (Mailer / Report)
========================================

- send_summaries builds @global_rows from @results and sends the
  global summary to the email: to= recipient, then groups devices by their
  to= override and sends each distinct override recipient one mail with
  only its own devices (dedup). The global recipient is never double-mailed.
- The mail is multipart: a multipart/related wrapping a
  multipart/alternative (a plain-text summary built by build_report_text
  -- the stats and device tables in text, no diffs -- and an HTML part built by
  build_report_html), plus the inline logo image. The MIME emission matches
  the fetchconfig-web mailer byte-structure (folded boundaries, CRLF, 12-byte
  random boundaries) so the inline logo renders.
- web_report_url is the base fetchconfig-web CGI URL; the mailer appends
  ?action=view_report&file=<basename> to build the "full report" button
  link.
- Report.pm writes the on-disk HTML change report (a summary table plus
  a coloured, masked diff per changed device) to the report directory; the
  mail embeds up to email_max_diff diffs and links to the full report.

