Extension recipes
=================

Concrete "how to add X". Each assumes you have read the relevant section above.

Add a device option
-------------------
1. Choose the accessor by type: directory-valued (register in
   %DIR_VALUED_OPTION), numeric (dev_option_posint), boolean
   (dev_option_flag), plain (dev_option). $dev_opt_tab -- the per-device option
hash.
2. Read it where it applies (a model's do_fetch, the pipeline, or a
   maintenance mode). No parser change is needed.
3. If it is universal, document it in the README "available for all models"
   block and the HTML matrix intro; if model-specific, in that model's
   section and matrix row.
4. Add a test under t/ (see Testing).

Add a new model
---------------
See Adding a model + How-To-add-a-model.

Add a maintenance/lookup mode
-----------------------------
1. Add the CLI option in fetchconfig.pl GetOptions and a dispatch block
   (model it on --list-allowed-dirs/--clean-lock-dir: it runs after
   phase-1 load, does its job on the stored backups, and exits). Add it to the
   $lookup_only set so no fetch phase runs.
2. Implement the work in Tools.pm, reusing its filesystem helpers
   (parse_backup_filename, sort_backups_desc, dir_is_empty, ...).
3. Pick an exit code consistent with Maintenance / lookup modes (Tools.pm);
   document in README + HTML + usage.
4. Add a test.

Add a per-device result field (e.g. a new mail column)
------------------------------------------------------
1. Pass it as a new record_result argument where the outcome is known
   (fetch_device). It is then automatically in @results, the .status
   file, and (under -P) the result sink -- see Parallel fetch (-P N).
2. Render it in build_report_html and build_report_text (keep the two in
   sync) and, if appropriate, in the statistics tally (row_state).
3. Add a test asserting it appears in both mail parts.

------------------------------------------------------------

Testing
=======

The test suite is in t/ (TAP, run with prove -I. t/ or make test).
Files are numbered by area: 00-compile (every model + the driver compile),
10/11/12/14 (text/quote/filename/option helpers), 30/31/32
(repository/compare/tools filesystem behaviour), 40/41 (template parser
and shipped templates), 42-44 (directory allowlist, system-dir deny,
allowlist abort), 45 (numeric option validation), 46/47 (locking,
skipped-state), 48/49/50 (web report URL, mail style, mail text),
51 (telnet debug).

Conventions for a new test:
- Prefer pure tests that exercise one function without a live device
  (mock Net::Telnet/Net::SMTP where needed; see t/46, t/49, t/51).
- Use File::Temp for filesystem tests.
- plan skip_all if a model needs a transport module that may be absent.

Sandbox vs AIX caveat: some tests require transport modules
(HTTP::Request::Common, Net::SNMP, Net::Telnet, ...) that may not be
installed on a development box. t/00-compile, t/32-tools, and
t/42-directory-allowlist in particular pull in the full model set. Run the
complete suite on the target (AIX) host, where all modules are present, before
tagging a release. On a bare dev box, test the pure helpers directly.

------------------------------------------------------------

Conventions and gotchas
=======================

- Phases are sacred. Never fetch in phase 1 (parse/register); never rely
  on parse-time side effects in phase 2.
- Models never abort the run. Return failure from do_fetch; let the
  pipeline record it and continue. Only table/allowlist errors abort, and only
  before fetching.
- Package my lexicals limit external testability. Several Mailer
  structures (%email_config, $rep_url, @results) are file-scoped
  lexicals; a test can only reach them through the module's own entry points
  (hence the small _test_set_rep hook used by the mail tests). If you add a
  structure that a test must drive, expose it through an entry point rather
  than expecting external assignment to work.
- -P isolates package state. A value a worker must return to the parent
  goes through record_result (the result sink), not a package variable
  (Parallel fetch (-P N)).
- MIME is byte-sensitive. The summary mail's MIME must match the
  fetchconfig-web mailer exactly (CRLF, folded boundaries, 12-byte random
  boundaries, base64 wrapped at 76). Do not "tidy" the send_mail emission.
- Secrets. Any new code that sends a credential uses print_secret; any
  new code that logs a device-table line uses mask_secrets; any new code
  that renders config content in a report uses mask_line.
- Timeouts and keep are validated. A non-positive-integer
  timeout/fetch_timeout/banner_timeout/prompt_settle_ms falls back to
  the default (via dev_option_posint/the dev_option numeric guard); a
  non-positive-integer keep is rejected at load time (it would otherwise
  delete every backup).
- Version/CHANGES discipline. Bump Constants.pm, add a CHANGES entry
  (<=80-char lines, LF-only), and keep README + the HTML manual in step with
  the code -- the HTML is generated from its .md via tools/md2html.py
  (run by make doc, which launches the Python tools through tools/pyrun:
  python3 on Linux, python on AIX).

------------------------------------------------------------

Backup-history DB writer (optional)
===================================

Off unless a db: line is set (default: level). It records two timelines to
PostgreSQL for a dashboard; the engine only writes, never issues DDL. Module:
fetchconfig/History.pm. DBI/DBD::Pg load only when db: is set. The .status
file is unchanged.

Two timelines (different cardinality):

- Run / health history: one row per device per run, success or not. Dense. Table
  backup_runs.
- Change history: one row per SAVED backup (with a sha1). Sparse. Table
  backup_versions. A per-device current snapshot is kept denormalized in
  backup_current.

Data path (parent only; never in -P workers):

1. During the fetch, nothing touches the DB. Detector::fetch_device records the
   run through Mailer::record_result, now also carrying size_bytes, path,
   file_timestamp and sha1 for a RETAINED backup (the metadata
   Abstract::dump_config stashes on the model instance as {saved}). Expiry
   emits Mailer::record_expire events. Under -P these ride the result sink and
   are replayed into the parent.
2. At end of run (fetchconfig.pl, after run_fetch_phase, before
   send_summaries), History::finish builds the ops from the replayed results
   and writes a per-repository WRITE-AHEAD spool file (<repo>/.db-<pid>-<ts>, a
   dotfile, temp+rename).
3. It then drains every spool file into the DB, oldest first, one transaction
   per
   file. Two runs may share a repository with no lock, so each file is first
   claimed by an atomic rename to <repo>/.db-<pid>-<ts>.draining.<drainer-pid>
   before it is read -- rename(2) lets exactly one drainer win a given file, so
a
   file is never applied twice concurrently (which also removes the
   inventory-component duplication hazard). A file is unlinked only after
COMMIT;
   on the first read/apply/commit failure the drainer rolls back, renames the
file
   back to its claimable name, and stops (that file and later ones wait for the
   next run). Because files are taken oldest-first, losing a claim means another
   drainer already holds the oldest-unhandled file, so this drainer stops rather
   than apply a newer file ahead of it -- preserving order across drainers
without
   a lock. Before each repo is scanned, _reclaim_orphans recovers any
   .draining.* file whose owner PID is dead (kill 0: ESRCH) by renaming it back
   to its claimable name, and drops any spool -- claimed or not -- older than
   db_max_outbox_days.
4. History::finish logs its own -----[DB writer]----- section (same 92-char
   width as the mail's -----[email]-----): the divider first, then any spool or
   drain warnings, then the result line History::status_line ("all N record(s)
   written; outbox empty", or the INCOMPLETE form). This runs before
   send_summaries and independent of "email:", so a no-mail run still reports
   the DB outcome. When "email:" is set, the main summary mail also renders a
   status box inside the card from History::mail_status (green = written, red =
   incomplete, grey = no db:); _db_status_box_html builds it and the per-device
   override mails omit it. Log and mail read the one %summary that finish set.
   db_sslmode defaults to disable (a local DB on the same box); set require
   or stronger for a DB across a WAN. Mail markup is Outlook-safe: the report
   button is a VML <v:roundrect> and the status pill a one-cell <td> badge,
   each behind [if mso] with the plain <a>/<span> behind [if !mso] (VML
   namespaces + an OfficeDocumentSettings DPI block are in build_report_html's
   <head>); the box title and pill are separate <td>s; and the summary tables
   use soft zebra shading (@ROW_BG) to separate rows instead of grid lines. Each
   cell sets its colour via both the bgcolor attribute and CSS (OWA's sanitizer
   can drop a cell's CSS background). The table uses border-collapse: collapse
   with no cell borders: that removes the sub-pixel inter-row gap Word would
   otherwise paint with the table background (the faint 1px seam it drew under
the
   darker band), so each row's own shade is all that shows - no single table or
   border colour has to stand in for both shades.
   send_mail sends the HTML part as quoted-printable (not 8bit): the markup
   has long lines (a table row is one line), and an MTA that folds a raw line
past
   the 998-octet SMTP limit corrupts the HTML mid-tag; QP soft-wraps at 76
chars.

Write-ahead means the records hit disk before the DB is touched, so a DB outage
only defers the drain (the run always completes) and nothing is lost. The claim
rename, the rename-back on failure, and the dead-PID orphan reclaim make the
drain
crash-safe too: a drainer that dies mid-write leaves its DB transaction
uncommitted (the backend rolls it back when the connection drops) and the spool
file intact under its .draining name, to be reclaimed and retried on a later
run.
Every op is idempotent (INSERT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS, keyed UPDATE/upsert), so
the
commit-then-crash re-apply window is a no-op. The PID-liveness reclaim assumes
one
host per repository -- the same assumption the .db-<pid>-<ts> naming already
makes; the db_max_outbox_days drop is the final backstop that clears any orphan
a
PID check cannot (e.g. a reused PID, or a host that cannot signal-probe).

--db-backfill (History::backfill) reconstructs the history from the backups
already on disk, for first enabling db: on an existing install. It writes
directly (not via the spool - this is a manual maintenance pass, not the fetch
path): it connects once (aborts if unreachable), and per device enumerates the
on-disk backups the same way -l does (device_info -> dev_option("repository")
-> scan_dir -> sort_backups_desc). For each backup it inserts a
backup_versions row - sha1 recomputed by _sha1_file, which hashes the file's
raw bytes and so is identical to the live save's add("$_\n") because the file IS
those bytes - and a backup_runs row (run_end = the file's file_timestamp from
backup_epoch_from_filename, state success, changed, duration NULL), both via
_apply_op; then one backup_current row (_current_row, from <dev_id>.status
plus the newest backup) for a device that has none. Every write is guarded by a
prior existence SELECT (_row_exists), so a re-run skips present rows and does
not re-hash, and a backup_current a live run wrote is never overwritten; work is
committed per device (rolled back on a DB error, which is counted and the run
continues). -T runs the same existence checks and counts but does no hashing and
no writes. The dense run history cannot be rebuilt (unchanged/failed runs leave
no
file), so only the saved change-events are added. It logs its own
-----[DB backfill]----- section (same width as -----[DB writer]-----): a
process: <path> debug line per backup, then the counts summary and a final
Success (or, on a failure, the error count).

Device-id rename on the outbox. --rename-device-id (Locking covers
the table lock) emits one op=rename record (old_dev_id/new_dev_id) into
the device's repository spool via History::queue_rename, then drains it
(History::replay) at the end of the command. _apply_op's rename kind runs
`UPDATE {backup_current,backup_runs,backup_versions} SET dev_id=new WHERE
dev_id=old in the file's transaction; it is idempotent (once applied, WHERE
dev_id=old matches nothing) and needs GRANT UPDATE (dev_id)` on the three tables
(fetchconfig-web side). If the DB is down the op simply stays in the outbox,
like
any other, and the next run's drain -- or an explicit --replay-db-change
(History::replay over every loaded device's repository, no fetch) -- applies it.
A live pre-check (row_count_for_dev) refuses a rename into an id that already
has
rows; if the DB is unreachable at rename time the apply-time transaction is the
backstop. When the inventory tables are present the rename also updates
device_inventory, device_inventory_component and device_inventory_history
(six UPDATEs rather than three).

SNMP device inventory (9.80, three tables owned by fetchconfig-web:
device_inventory, device_inventory_component, device_inventory_history).
These ride the same outbox as the backup history. Mailer::record_inventory /
record_inventory_component (parent, or replayed from the -P sink) collect the
per-device result; History::finish turns it into ops and _apply_op handles the
new kinds: op=inv_current upserts the device_inventory row (capturing the
prior hw_set/os_version first), op=inv_reset clears the device's
components (a snapshot replace), and op=inv_component inserts each component
(is_primary as 't'/'f'), including its firmware/ROMMON rev in fw_version
(web schema 2.16); op=inv_current likewise carries the device's own
sysname/syslocation/syscontact (web schema 2.17). _probe_inv_tables checks,
once, whether each added column exists (via pg_attribute/pg_class, which can't
poison a transaction): the component INSERT omits fw_version when
device_inventory_component.fw_version is absent, and the inv_current upsert
omits
the three sys* columns when device_inventory.sysname is absent, so the engine
runs
against a 2.15, 2.16 or 2.17 DB without a 42703 (the sys* fields are
current-state
only -- never part of change detection). The current-row logic is tri-state: a
disabled
status is UPDATE-only (never inserts), a failed attempt keeps the existing data
(only the status/timestamps move), and a successful attempt upserts everything,
conditionally advances changed_at, and -- derived during the drain, not stored
on
the result -- inserts a device_inventory_history row (`INSERT ... WHERE NOT
EXISTS, note = initial/members/os/both) only when hw_set or os_version`
actually changed. Every inventory op is gated behind a one-time pre-check
(_probe_inv_tables, a pg_class lookup -- no to_regclass, which is 9.4+): when
the tables are absent _apply_op returns success without touching them, so an
engine pointed at a web side not yet upgraded is a no-op. All DML is
8.2-safe and uses DBI placeholders, exactly like the backup-history ops.

Per-version line deltas (lines_added/lines_removed, web 2.12). These are the
+added/-removed of Abstract::line_delta (a dependency-free diff: trim the common
prefix/suffix, then an LCS-length DP; added = curr - LCS, removed = prev - LCS)
over $mod->config_norm_lines, which returns the file as the
ignore/canon-normalized
line list - the SAME view config_equal compares, via the new config_ignore_re /
config_canon model hooks (CiscoASA's constant regex, GenericTemplate's
cur_ignore_re/cur_canon, base = raw). The live writer computes the delta in
Detector::fetch_device (previous stored config vs the just-saved one) and
carries
it on the result into the version op; the oldest/first version is the baseline
(NULL). backfill walks a device oldest->newest keeping the previous normalized
lines, and either inserts a new row with the delta or UPDATEs an existing row
whose
lines_added is still NULL. The columns are owned by fetchconfig-web; _connect
probes information_schema once ($delta_cols) and the writes include the two
columns only when present, so the engine stays compatible with a web side not
yet
upgraded. Parity note: a GenericTemplate device has no template loaded under
backfill, so its cur_ignore_re is undef and its backfilled delta is over raw
lines - accepted; live runs (template loaded) count the normalized view.

Pre-9.5 PostgreSQL (production is 8.2.23): no ON CONFLICT/MERGE/jsonb.
Append-only
inserts use INSERT ... SELECT ... WHERE NOT EXISTS (<key>); backup_current is
UPDATE-else-INSERT (a successful run advances last_good_at/last_good_size, a
failed run leaves them untouched; two UPDATE shapes avoid a boolean bind in a
CASE). All DML uses DBI placeholders. The writer role needs SELECT as well as
INSERT/UPDATE on the three tables, because the idempotency subqueries and the
UPDATE WHERE/value expressions read them; that grant is created by
fetchconfig-web
(fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl --upgrade), not by the engine.

All DB timestamps (run_end, last_good_at, expired_at, file_timestamp) use
one convention: the box's LOCAL wall-clock encoded as seconds (via timegm of the
local parts), not a true UTC instant. fetchconfig and its fetchconfig-web run on
a
single box in a single timezone, so the stored values are directly the local
time
and fetchconfig-web renders them as-is, with no timezone conversion.
file_timestamp is derived from the same parts at save and at expiry
(Abstract::backup_epoch_from_parts), so the keyed row matches; the filename tz
token is ignored, and timegm does not consult the host zone. (Spool file-age
pruning uses real elapsed time, independent of this display convention.)

